msgid "strtok_r - string tokenize reentrant"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<char *strtok_r(char *_Nullable restrict >I<str>B<, const char *restrict "
">I<delim>B<,>\n"
"B<               char **restrict >I<saveptr>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:28
msgid "B<strtok_r>():"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:31
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    _POSIX_C_SOURCE\n"
"        || /* glibc E<lt>= 2.19: */ _BSD_SOURCE || _SVID_SOURCE\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:37
msgid "The B<strtok_r>()  function is a reentrant version of B<strtok>(3)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:46
msgid ""
"The I<saveptr> argument is a pointer to a I<char\\~*> variable that is used "
"internally by B<strtok_r>()  in order to maintain context between successive "
"calls that parse the same string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:59
msgid ""
"On the first call to B<strtok_r>(), I<str> should point to the string to be "
"parsed, and the value of I<*saveptr> is ignored (but see VERSIONS).  In "
"subsequent calls, I<str> should be NULL, and I<saveptr> (and the buffer that "
"it points to)  should be unchanged since the previous call."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:65
msgid ""
"Different strings may be parsed concurrently using sequences of calls to "
"B<strtok_r>()  that specify different I<saveptr> arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:69
msgid ""
"B<strtok_r>()  returns a pointer to the next token, or NULL if there are no "
"more tokens."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<strtok_r>()"
msgstr ""

#.  Tru64, according to its manual page
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:91
msgid ""
"On some implementations, I<*saveptr> is required to be NULL on the first "
"call to B<strtok_r>()  that is being used to parse I<str>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:104
msgid ""
"The program below uses nested loops that employ B<strtok_r>()  to break a "
"string into a two-level hierarchy of tokens.  The first command-line "
"argument specifies the string to be parsed.  The second argument specifies "
"the delimiter byte(s)  to be used to separate that string into \"major\" "
"tokens.  The third argument specifies the delimiter byte(s)  to be used to "
"separate the \"major\" tokens into subtokens."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:106
msgid "An example of the output produced by this program is the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$B< ./a.out \\[aq]a/bbb///cc;xxx:yyy:\\[aq] \\[aq]:;\\[aq] \\[aq]/\\[aq]>\n"
"1: a/bbb///cc\n"
"         --E<gt> a\n"
"         --E<gt> bbb\n"
"         --E<gt> cc\n"
"2: xxx\n"
"         --E<gt> xxx\n"
"3: yyy\n"
"         --E<gt> yyy\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strtok_r.3:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include E<lt>stdio.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>stdlib.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>string.hE<gt>\n"
"\\&\n"
"int\n"
"main(int argc, char *argv[])\n"
"{\n"
"    char *str1, *str2, *token, *subtoken;\n"
"    char *saveptr1, *saveptr2;\n"
"    int j;\n"
"\\&\n"
"    if (argc != 4) {\n"
"        fprintf(stderr, \"Usage: %s string delim subdelim\\[rs]n\",\n"
"                argv[0]);\n"
"        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"    }\n"
"\\&\n"
"    for (j = 1, str1 = argv[1]; ; j++, str1 = NULL) {\n"
"        token = strtok_r(str1, argv[2], &saveptr1);\n"
"        if (token == NULL)\n"
"            break;\n"
"        printf(\"%d: %s\\[rs]n\", j, token);\n"
"\\&\n"
"        for (str2 = token; ; str2 = NULL) {\n"
"            subtoken = strtok_r(str2, argv[3], &saveptr2);\n"
"            if (subtoken == NULL)\n"
"                break;\n"
"            printf(\"\\[rs]t --E<gt> %s\\[rs]n\", subtoken);\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"\\&\n"
"    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:8
#, no-wrap
msgid "strverscmp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:11
msgid "strverscmp - compare two version strings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<int strverscmp(const char *>I<s1>B<, const char *>I<s2>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#.  classical solution: "rename jan jan0 jan?"
#.  sort -V sorts a-1.0a < a-1.0.1a; strverscmp() does not
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:41
msgid ""
"For a dataset like I<jan1>, I<jan2>, ..., I<jan9>, I<jan10>, ...  sorting it "
"lexicographically yields I<jan1>, I<jan10>, ..., I<jan2>, ..., I<jan9>.  The "
"task of B<strverscmp>()  is to compare two strings yielding the former "
"order, while B<strcmp>(3)  finds only the lexicographic order.  This "
"function does not use the locale category B<LC_COLLATE>, so is meant mostly "
"for situations where the strings are expected to be in ASCII.  This is "
"different from the ordering produced by B<sort>(1)  B<-V>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:59
msgid ""
"What this function does is the following.  If both strings are equal, return "
"0.  Otherwise, find the position between two bytes with the property that "
"before it both strings are equal, while directly after it there is a "
"difference.  Find the largest consecutive digit strings containing (or "
"starting at, or ending at) this position.  If one or both of these is empty, "
"then return what B<strcmp>(3)  would have returned (numerical ordering of "
"byte values).  Otherwise, compare both digit strings numerically, where "
"digit strings with one or more leading zeros are interpreted as if they have "
"a decimal point in front (so that in particular digit strings with more "
"leading zeros come before digit strings with fewer leading zeros).  Thus, "
"the ordering is I<000>, I<00>, I<01>, I<010>, I<09>, I<0>, I<1>, I<9>, "
"I<10>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:68
msgid ""
"The B<strverscmp>()  function returns an integer less than, equal to, or "
"greater than zero if I<s1> is found, respectively, to be earlier than, equal "
"to, or later than I<s2>."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<strverscmp>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:101
msgid ""
"The program below can be used to demonstrate the behavior of "
"B<strverscmp>().  It uses B<strverscmp>()  to compare the two strings given "
"as its command-line arguments.  An example of its use is the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:106
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$B< ./a.out jan1 jan10>;\n"
"jan1 E<lt> jan10\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:134
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define _GNU_SOURCE\n"
"#include E<lt>stdio.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>stdlib.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>string.hE<gt>\n"
"\\&\n"
"int\n"
"main(int argc, char *argv[])\n"
"{\n"
"    int res;\n"
"\\&\n"
"    if (argc != 3) {\n"
"        fprintf(stderr, \"Usage: %s E<lt>string1E<gt> "
"E<lt>string2E<gt>\\[rs]n\", argv[0]);\n"
"        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"    }\n"
"\\&\n"
"    res = strverscmp(argv[1], argv[2]);\n"
"\\&\n"
"    printf(\"%s %s %s\\[rs]n\", argv[1],\n"
"           (res E<lt> 0) ? \"E<lt>\" : (res == 0) ? \"==\" : \"E<gt>\", "
"argv[2]);\n"
"\\&\n"
"    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strverscmp.3:140
msgid "B<rename>(1), B<strcasecmp>(3), B<strcmp>(3), B<strcoll>(3)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "strxfrm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:10
msgid "strxfrm - string transformation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<size_t strxfrm(>size_t size;\n"
"B<               char >I<dest>B<[restrict >I<size>B<], const char "
">I<src>B<[restrict >I<size>B<],>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<size>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:44
msgid ""
"The B<strxfrm>()  function transforms the I<src> string into a form such "
"that the result of B<strcmp>(3)  on two strings that have been transformed "
"with B<strxfrm>()  is the same as the result of B<strcoll>(3)  on the two "
"strings before their transformation.  The first I<size> bytes of the "
"transformed string are placed in I<dest>.  The transformation is based on "
"the program's current locale for category B<LC_COLLATE>.  (See "
"B<setlocale>(3))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:58
msgid ""
"The B<strxfrm>()  function returns the number of bytes required to store the "
"transformed string in I<dest> excluding the terminating null byte "
"(\\[aq]\\[rs]0\\[aq]).  If the value returned is I<size> or more, the "
"contents of I<dest> are indeterminate."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/strxfrm.3:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<strxfrm>()"
msgstr ""

